  <h2>The six skills</h2>
  <table>
    <thead><tr><th>Skill</th><th>What it does</th><th>Where a human decides</th></tr></thead>
    <tbody>
      <tr><td><code>/research:preflight</code></td><td>Checks for latexmk, a TeX distribution, the venue's font packages, Node 18+ and pdfinfo. Only git is a hard stop.</td><td>Every install is offered one command at a time, with its download size. Nothing installs without a yes.</td></tr>
      <tr><td><code>/research:establish</code></td><td>Interviews you, writes the per-repo delta, scaffolds the layout, installs build scripts and CI, seeds the memory bank, and confirms the skeleton compiles. Upgrades an existing repo by diffing, never overwriting.</td><td>The interview asks about the research first (topic, core claim, state of the work, audience) and then the paperwork. Any question can be skipped, and the skill may not invent a project brief. It asks before creating a remote or pushing.</td></tr>
      <tr><td><code>/research:audit</code></td><td>Reports drift from the pinned version, stale or template-text memory files, citation-matrix drift, and anything that blocks submission.</td><td>Report only.</td></tr>
      <tr><td><code>/research:add-paper</code></td><td>Fetches one reference by DOI, arXiv id or URL, verifies it against Crossref, arXiv or OpenAlex, and writes the BibTeX and matrix entry.</td><td>It refuses to invent an entry it can't verify, which leaves the call to you.</td></tr>
      <tr><td><code>/research:citation-matrix</code></td><td>Verifies every cited key: existence, author, title, year and venue against the scholarly APIs; flags preprints, undisclosed self-citations, orphans and missing entries.</td><td>Flagged items go to the author.</td></tr>
      <tr><td><code>/research:originality-check</code></td><td>Looks for verbatim and close-paraphrase overlap with your sources and cited works, self-plagiarism against your own prior papers, unmarked quotations, and AI-drafted text you haven't verified.</td><td>A report for you to judge, never pass or fail. It has no access to published corpora and cannot certify originality.</td></tr>
    </tbody>
  </table>

  <h2>The eight agents</h2>
  <table>
    <thead><tr><th>Agent</th><th>Commands</th></tr></thead>
    <tbody>
      <tr><td><code>paper-agent</code></td><td>outline, survey, draft, revise, create-sprint, validate, camera-ready</td></tr>
      <tr><td><code>proposal-agent</code></td><td>analyze-cfp, design, update-design, create-sprint, validate. Nothing is written without an approved design.</td></tr>
      <tr><td><code>position-paper-agent</code></td><td>thesis, arguments, survey, draft, stress-test, validate</td></tr>
      <tr><td><code>latex-agent</code></td><td>compile, init, check-refs, format-bib, page-count, fix-warnings</td></tr>
      <tr><td><code>citation-agent</code></td><td>build, verify, add, check, sync. Owns the citation matrix.</td></tr>
      <tr><td><code>originality-agent</code></td><td>review, self-check, unverified</td></tr>
      <tr><td><code>review-agent</code></td><td>review, verify-citations, compile-check, update-matrix. The merge-gate report.</td></tr>
      <tr><td><code>memory-agent</code></td><td>update, archive, validate, status, sync-phases</td></tr>
    </tbody>
  </table>
  <p>
    Every agent reads its paths from the repository's research delta instead of hardcoding them. That one
    change is what collapsed the copied variants into one installed set.
  </p>

  <h2>What the checker enforces</h2>
  <p>
    <code>research-checks.mjs</code> is plain Node and runs the same way for a person, an agent or CI. It
    fails the build on: a missing or unpinned plugin version, unfilled delta placeholders, placeholder text
    (TODO, TBD, FIXME) in the paper, a <code>\cite</code> with no bibliography entry, undefined references
    after compiling, author details left in a double-blind submission, and a delta that disagrees with
    whether a governance delta is present. It warns on orphan bibliography entries, cited keys missing from
    the citation matrix, and build output left beside the source.
  </p>

  <h2>With agentic-governance</h2>
  <p>
    The plugin is standalone. When a repo also runs <a href={`${project}how-governance-works/`}>agentic-governance</a>,
    <code>establish</code> is supposed to defer to the governance delta for the memory-bank path and print
    allowlist lines that keep the paper out of the L0 lane. Paper prose is semantic work, so an agent should
    never be able to merge writing without human review.
  </p>

  <h2>Known gaps</h2>
  <p>
    Found by reading the source on 9 November 2026. These are the reasons for the
    <a href={`${project}completeness-benchmark/`}>completeness benchmark</a>.
  </p>
  <ul class="gaps">
    <li>
      <strong>Composition looks in the wrong place.</strong> <code>establish</code> and the checker look
      for <code>docs/governance-delta.md</code>. Since governance v0.3 that file lives at
      <code>llm/governance/governance-delta.md</code>. On a current repo, research would conclude governance is
      absent and skip the deny lines that keep paper prose out of the L0 lane. This is a prediction from the
      source; the benchmark's first run is set up to confirm or refute it.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>Its own delta is in the data plane.</strong> <code>docs/research-delta.md</code> governs the repo,
      so under governance's ADR-0001 it belongs in <code>llm/</code>.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>It assumes Claude Code.</strong> The skills use Claude-specific tools for asking the user and for
      locating plugin files, and they are invoked as slash commands. The 1 October audit maps the separation
      needed for other harnesses. It hasn't been implemented.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>It supports writing far more than research.</strong> The source never mentions hypotheses,
      reproducibility, pre-registration, ethics review or responding to reviewers. Experiment and analysis
      support amounts to optional <code>experiments/</code>, <code>data/</code> and <code>results/</code>
      folders. That came from a keyword search, which is a blunt instrument. Measuring it properly is the
      point of the benchmark.
    </li>
  </ul>

